course: dictionary
lesson: 7075
title: Names from fathers
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A patronymic is a name made from the name of your father or another male ancestor, such as your grandfather. It tells you whose child you are. In Ukrainian, «Олена Петрівна» means Olena, daughter of Petro, because Petrivna is made from the father's name Petro.
explain: Many English surnames began as patronymics. Jones means son of John, and Robertson means son of Robert. Look for a father's name hidden inside a surname, and you may have found a patronymic.
explain: Names from other countries work in similar ways. In Scotland, Mac means son, so MacDonald means son of Donald. In Ireland, the Ó in O'Brien means descendant. In Iceland, «Björk Guðmundsdóttir» means Björk, daughter of Guðmundur, because dóttir means daughter.
fact: A patronymic is a name made from a father's name or a male ancestor's name.
fact: As an adjective, patronymic describes a name made from a father's name.
fact: Jones means son of John, and Williams means son of William.
fact: In Ukrainian and Russian, a patronymic is a middle name, such as Petrivna, meaning daughter of Petro.
fact: English pupils usually say Mr, Mrs, Ms or Miss with the surname, while Ukrainians may use the first name and patronymic.
exercise: In «Олена Петрівна», what does Петрівна mean? | Wife of Olena | Teacher of the class | *Daughter of Petro | Olena's best friend
exercise: Which surname means son of John? | Green | Brown | Smith | *Jones
exercise: What does Mac mean in MacDonald? | Red | *Son | Hill | Farm
test: In O'Brien, what does the Irish Ó suggest? | Friend of | *Descendant of | Born in | Hair of
test: Is Johnson a patronymic? | No, it is a job | Yes, it comes from a colour | No, it is a place name | *Yes, it comes from the name John
exam: In «Ivanovych», whose name is hidden inside? | Petro | Olena | Guðmundur | *Ivan
